{"id":"n-20261029-1k3amm","category":"health","display_title":"Scotland Health Board Medicine Variation","display_title_he":"שונות בתרופות בין מנהלות הבריאות בסקוטלנד","short_summary":"A new report reveals striking regional disparities in how Scottish health boards prescribe new medicines, including weight loss and diabetes drugs.","english_level":"C1","words":469,"reading_time":3,"image":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/75/Dumfries_and_Galloway_Health_Board_Building%2C_Nithbank_-_geograph.org.uk_-_455788.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Darrin Antrobus · CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dumfries_and_Galloway_Health_Board_Building,_Nithbank_-_geograph.org.uk_-_455788.jpg"},"sources":[{"name":"BBC News","domain":"bbc.co.uk","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c98rz4g0zgldo","title":"NHS Western Isles prescribing weight loss drugs at triple the rate of other health boards","published_at":"2026-10-29T06:13:11.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-29T06:13:11.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-29T07:16:21.586Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":8,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["A recent report published by the Association of the British Pharmaceutical Industry has highlighted significant geographic disparities in the distribution of new medicines across Scotland. According to the findings, patients residing in the Western Isles are receiving prescriptions for diabetes and weight management medications at a rate nearly three times higher than any other regional health board in the country. Analysts examined prescribing data spanning from September 2020 through August 2025, uncovering substantial variations in patient access to approved treatments such as GLP-1 medications and therapies for cardiovascular conditions.","The analysis focused on a three-month average quantity of specific drugs prescribed per head of population between June and August 2025. NHS Western Isles recorded the highest rate at 1,086.54, followed by NHS Greater Glasgow and Clyde at 392.97, whereas NHS Shetland registered the lowest rate at 100.31. However, report authors noted certain limitations in the data, explaining that small island populations can experience skewed per capita rates because a small number of individual prescriptions can significantly alter the overall statistical metrics.","Additional media investigations revealed that fewer than half of Scotland's fourteen territorial health boards were actively prescribing GLP-1 medications specifically for weight loss, with fewer than eight hundred Scottish residents receiving the injections for that indication. Medical professionals, including representatives from the Royal College of Physicians, criticized the limited availability, arguing that restricted access to these cost-effective treatments is discriminatory against obese individuals and prevents potential savings on obesity-related illnesses.","NHS Western Isles currently has 340 patients enrolled on weight loss medication programs, representing the highest figure among the six health boards participating in official pilot schemes. Officials from NHS Western Isles explained that they established an early structured clinical pathway for GLP-1 access ahead of other regions. They emphasized that their high prescribing rates reflect this proactive implementation and local clinical need rather than participation in the pilot alone, noting that the drugs are paired with dietetic-led lifestyle and behavioral support.","The pharmaceutical industry report also examined non-statin cholesterol-lowering medicines intended for cardiovascular disease, which affects approximately 800,000 individuals across Scotland as a leading cause of mortality. Prescribing rates for these heart-related treatments differed by eight to tenfold between the highest board, Highland, and the lowest board, Forth Valley. Researchers observed that regions featuring stronger professional collaboration between general practitioners and medical specialists generally achieved higher prescribing volumes.","Responding to the findings, Victoria Jordan, the executive director of patient access at the ABPI, stated that while Scotland possesses a robust system for evaluating medicines, avoidable administrative barriers often delay patient access to treatments already deemed safe and cost-effective. The pharmaceutical association issued seven core recommendations, urging enhanced cooperation, strategic planning, and greater transparency from health boards regarding local decision-making processes. Meanwhile, the Scottish government indicated that it would carefully review the report as part of broader upcoming health service reforms aimed at standardizing regional access."],"facts":[{"fact":"Patients in the Western Isles are being prescribed drugs used for weight loss and diabetes at almost three times the rate of any other health board in Scotland.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"A new report from the Association of the British Pharmaceutical Industry (ABPI) found significant variation between Scotland's health boards prescribing new medicines, including GLP-1 medications and cardiovascular treatments.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"NHS Western Isles prescribed the highest rate of GLP-1 medication at 1,086.54 per head of population between June and August 2025, while NHS Shetland had the lowest at 100.31.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Fewer than half of Scotland's 14 territorial health boards were prescribing GLP-1s specifically for weight loss, and fewer than 800 Scots had received the jabs for this purpose so far.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"NHS Western Isles is one of six health boards taking part in pilot programmes to improve access to weight loss medication and stated it established a structured pathway early.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The Scottish government stated it would carefully consider the findings of the report as part of its health board reforms.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"Association of the British Pharmaceutical Industry","lemma":"Association of the British Pharmaceutical Industry","pos":"name","he":"איגוד תעשיית התרופות הבריטית","name":true},{"text":"ABPI","lemma":"ABPI","pos":"name","he":"איגוד תעשיית התרופות הבריטית","name":true},{"text":"Scotland","lemma":"Scotland","pos":"name","he":"סקוטלנד","name":true},{"text":"Western Isles","lemma":"Western Isles","pos":"name","he":"האיים המערביים","name":true},{"text":"Greater Glasgow and Clyde","lemma":"Greater Glasgow and Clyde","pos":"name","he":"גלאזגו רבתי וקלייד","name":true},{"text":"Shetland","lemma":"Shetland","pos":"name","he":"שטלנד","name":true},{"text":"Highland","lemma":"Highland","pos":"name","he":"היילנד","name":true},{"text":"Forth Valley","lemma":"Forth Valley","pos":"name","he":"פורת' ואלי","name":true},{"text":"Victoria Jordan","lemma":"Victoria Jordan","pos":"name","he":"ויקטוריה ג'ורדן","name":true},{"text":"Royal College of Physicians","lemma":"Royal College of Physicians","pos":"name","he":"הקולג' המלכותי של הרופאים","name":true},{"text":"NHS Western Isles","lemma":"NHS Western Isles","pos":"name","he":"שירות הבריאות הלאומי של האיים המערביים","name":true},{"text":"recent","lemma":"recent","pos":"adjective","he":"אחרון","name":false},{"text":"report","lemma":"report","pos":"noun","he":"דוח","name":false},{"text":"publish","lemma":"publish","pos":"verb","he":"לפרסם","name":false},{"text":"highlight","lemma":"highlight","pos":"verb","he":"להדגיש","name":false},{"text":"significant","lemma":"significant","pos":"adjective","he":"משמעותי","name":false},{"text":"geographic","lemma":"geographic","pos":"adjective","he":"גאוגרפי","name":false},{"text":"disparity","lemma":"disparity","pos":"noun","he":"פער","name":false},{"text":"distribution","lemma":"distribution","pos":"noun","he":"חלוקה","name":false},{"text":"medicine","lemma":"medicine","pos":"noun","he":"תרופה","name":